@@ -1205,7 +1205,7 @@ KFlash's knobs are `NI_KFLASH_<NAME>`; each was `NI_DFLASH_<NAME>` before #362 a
 | `NI_SHORTPF_EXACT` | on | A short prefill pass (at most 128 rows: a cached turn's new message, the reply the server prefills while idle, a draft read ahead) gives each row the same bits whatever the pass's length, so reading a prompt's tail in several such passes gives the logits one pass gives (a follow-up after the idle history prefill replies as with `NI_HISTORY_PREFILL=0`). The short path's choices that followed the row count are fixed: each projection group keeps one GEMM family (`qmms` over up to 128 rows in one dispatch, the qmr tiles for the 27B's large groups; `NI_SHORTPF_EXACT_GEMM=qmms` or `tile` forces one), the 8-bit router `qmms`, the routed experts `qmms_moe3` (`qmms_moe2`'s FMA arithmetic for every expert: an MMA that reproduces it from 3 rows), attention's keys split at fixed tiles (`NI_SHORTPF_EXACT_SEG`, 32 tiles of 32 keys) through `attn_prefill11gs` even for one split. Passes above 128 rows run the large-prefill kernels, whose sums are ordered otherwise, so a tail read across that bound can still part at a near-tie; on an M5 so can a dense model's pass across 64 rows (`NI_SP_QMT`), or across 17 for the 27B on the M5 Max (`NI_QMT_MIN`). `0` restores the row-count choices. Cost on the 35B-A3B (MacBook Pro M4 Pro): a pass of 1-16 rows after a 16K prefix the same, 32-64 rows +4-7%, 116-128 rows +5.5%; cold prefill and decode unchanged (their passes are not short). |
 | `NI_SP_QMT` | `1` | `0` restores the earlier preference. A chunk of 64 to 128 rows (from `NI_QMT_MIN` rows) whose projections the tensor-op GEMM covers - 4-bit group 64, `K` a multiple of 64, a dense model - runs on the 64-row-tile `qmt` / `qmtg` kernels rather than the short-prefill GEMMs, which are built for chunks of at most 64 rows: one weights read through register tiles, cheaper than the tile kernels only while that read is one tile's. At a 16K cached prefix it makes a 73-row chunk 14% and a 124-row one 33% faster, and its output gate (one 27B and one 4B, `logit_dump` at a 128-row chunk, `NI_SP_QMT=0` against the default) passes both models' floor files on all three decode paths with no top-1 flip and a mean KL of 3.4e-07 (27B) and 6.3e-06 (4B, PR #366). MoE expert GEMMs and every MoE model keep the short-prefill tiles whatever this says. |
 | `NI_QMT_MIN` | 17 for the Qwen3.8 27B on an M5 Max (an Apple10 GPU of 40 cores or more), 64 elsewhere | The fewest rows of a dense 4-bit group-64 GEMM that run on the 64-row tensor-op tile (`qmt_m64_n64`, `qmtg_m64_n64`; it loads the rows past the pass's as zeros) instead of the short-prefill or `qmr` kernels; 1-64. A prompt's last chunk and a short turn's message are such passes. On the 27B on the M5 Max (`bench_qmm`) the tile runs gate/up 514 against 638 us at 17 rows and 527 against 1,215 at 33, down 337 against 623 at 33; at 16 rows and fewer the short tiles stay faster. A prompt of 33 ids prefills in 87.6 instead of 171.1 ms, 48 ids in 89.3 instead of 189.5, and the harness's 4K length (4,129 tokens: 2,048 + 2,048 + a 33-row tail) in 3,182 instead of 3,265 ms (-2.5%; interleaved A/B, M5 Max, macOS 26.4, `bench/results/2026-11-05-m5-max-qmt-short`). Its output check (`logit_dump --chunk 48`, every prefill pass on the tile) passes the 27B's M5 floor with no top-1 flip and a prefill mean KL of 1.5e-7: the same products, summed in another K order. Other models and Macs keep 64 today. |
-| `NI_ANE` | legacy inputs on; new inputs in fast | `0` keeps prefill on the GPU. Dense Qwen models with hidden size divisible by 2048 up to 8192 become eligible under `--profile fast` / `KAPITSA_PROFILE=fast`; `NI_ANE=1` explicitly enables them and `NI_ANE=0` overrides either profile. The default precise profile keeps these new widths on the GPU; existing 2560/5120 inputs keep their prior defaults. The existing 2560-channel geometry takes precedence where it fits, otherwise inputs use 2048-channel segments. All supported widths use the existing GPU input rotation; widths above 8192 remain on the GPU. Chip/calibration, weight format, memory, chunk and startup-check requirements still apply; unsupported cases remain on the GPU. This W8A8 approximation changes logits and can change greedy replies. Newly supported widths have a separate SSD prefix-cache namespace from precise, `NI_ANE=0` and older GPU-only builds when enabled. See [numerical history](numerical-history.md#ane-prefill-for-hidden-sizes-divisible-by-2048) for measured scope and limits. |
+| `NI_ANE` | legacy inputs on; new inputs in fast on non-matrix Macs | `0` keeps prefill on the GPU. Dense Qwen models with hidden size divisible by 2048 up to 8192 become eligible on non-matrix Macs under `--profile fast` / `KAPITSA_PROFILE=fast`; M5 and other matrix-unit Macs retain the GPU default for these new widths; `NI_ANE=1` explicitly enables them and `NI_ANE=0` overrides either profile. The default precise profile keeps these new widths on the GPU; existing 2560/5120 inputs keep their prior defaults. The existing 2560-channel geometry takes precedence where it fits, otherwise inputs use 2048-channel segments. All supported widths use the existing GPU input rotation; widths above 8192 remain on the GPU. M4 built-in ANE shares or explicit calibration on other non-matrix Macs (such as M3) are still required. Chip/calibration, weight format, memory, chunk and startup-check requirements still apply; unsupported cases remain on the GPU. This W8A8 approximation changes logits and can change greedy replies. Newly supported widths have a separate SSD prefix-cache namespace from precise, `NI_ANE=0` and older GPU-only builds when enabled. See [numerical history](numerical-history.md#ane-prefill-for-hidden-sizes-divisible-by-2048) for measured scope and limits. |
 | `NI_ANE_MMA` | on for the Qwen 4B and 27B | M5 with macOS 26, 4-bit group-64 Qwen dense models: the Neural Engine split's default share for the GPU's FFN on the matrix units (27B: 14 of 34 units on the 40-core M5 Max, 22 on the 20-core M5 Pro; 4B: 8 of 18 and 12). The per-unit costs were measured on the M5 Pro, then scaled by the 128-row tile's GEMM speedups (`NI_QMT128`); the M5 Max's 14 was measured best (27B 4K prefill -12.7% against 16, [results](../bench/results/2026-11-04-m5-max-ane-share-qmt128/README.md)), the M5 Pro's 22 is derived, not measured. Other models (the 9B) keep the older default share today. `1` takes it on any such model, `0` keeps the older one. This moves the share only: whenever the GPU keeps channels (any share below all units, a calibration, `NI_ANE_UNITS`), they run on the tensor-ops kernel, so the 9B and `0` are not fully pre-#318. |
 | `NI_ANE_SPLIT_WAIT_MS` | `60000` | A split chunk whose Neural Engine work has not finished within this many ms gives up the split: the log says the bound ran out and that the chunk reruns on the GPU, and the rest of the process stays on the GPU. A job that started and never reported is retired by its own 2 s watchdog first; this bound is the backstop for a chunk stuck before that. `tests/test_ane_split_abort.py` runs it at 250 ms with an injected fault. |
 | `NI_ANE_KEEP_S` | `300` | Seconds after the last prefill chunk (or model load) during which a background evaluation keeps the Neural Engine awake, so a short prompt after a pause skips its 70–120 ms wake. `0` turns it off. |
